            ":knowledge-point/canonical-example": {
              ":db/id": 17592186054455,
              ":question/id": "67ce214a-e9e9-5a32-b48c-d9488cb141ac",
              ":question/math-academy-id": "e-134",
              ":question/problem": "Find $f'(x)$ when $f(x)=x^3$.",
              ":question/worked-solution": "The power rule states that if\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^n\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf'(x) = nx^{n-1}\n$$\n\nFor the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=x^3\n$$\n\nwe have $n=3$. So\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= 3 \\cdot x^{3 - 1} = 3x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"
            },
            ":knowledge-point/id": "32dd6880-3725-564a-9e85-0d39b6960c72",
            ":knowledge-point/title": "Computing the Derivative of a Power Function Using the Power Rule"
          },
          {
            ":db/id": 17592186054811,
            ":knowledge-point/canonical-example": {
              ":db/id": 17592186054456,
              ":question/id": "54c18c81-9deb-5958-81a0-1abecf91ba58",
              ":question/math-academy-id": "e-135",
              ":question/problem": "Calculate the derivative of $f(x)=\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$.",
              ":question/worked-solution": "Here, we can rewrite the function as\n\n$$\nf(x)= \\dfrac{1}{x^2} = x^{-2}\n$$\n\nApplying the power rule with $n=-2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= (-2) \\cdot x^{-2 - 1} \\\\\n&=-2x^{-3} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2}{x^{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"
            },
            ":knowledge-point/id": "f2ff1808-d239-5625-93b7-21a98ada3fab",
            ":knowledge-point/title": "Computing the Derivative of a Negative Power Function Using the Power Rule"
          },
          {
            ":db/id": 17592186054812,
            ":knowledge-point/canonical-example": {
              ":db/id": 17592186054457,
              ":question/id": "1717a513-6866-5b4e-8c9a-1ea7f343acab",
              ":question/math-academy-id": "e-136",
              ":question/problem": "Find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ when $y=\\sqrt{x}$.",
              ":question/worked-solution": "Writing the radical as a rational exponent, we have\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{x}=x^{1/2}\n$$\n\nApplying the power rule with\n\n$$\nn=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{1/2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}x^{1/2 - 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}x^{-1/2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"
            },
            ":knowledge-point/id": "d8d06cd8-9aae-50ab-90dc-6c70b545d569",
            ":knowledge-point/title": "Computing the Derivative of a Fractional Power Function Using the Power Rule"
          },
